| Topic              | Explanation                                                                                                                                |
| ------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |
| Traditional Spring | Needed XML or Java-based configuration for beans, ViewResolvers, DataSources, etc.                                                         |
| Spring Boot        | Opinionated framework built on Spring → “Convention over Configuration”. Automatically configures beans based on classpath and properties. |
| Embedded server    | No external Tomcat needed — Boot embeds Tomcat/Jetty.                                                                                      |
| Starters           | Simplify dependencies — e.g. `spring-boot-starter-web`, `spring-boot-starter-data-jpa`.                                                    |
| Main class         | Uses `@SpringBootApplication` ( = `@Configuration` + `@EnableAutoConfiguration` + `@ComponentScan`)                                        |


| Term                              | Meaning                                          |
| --------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------ |
| **Spring Boot**                   | Framework to *bootstrap* Spring apps quickly     |
| **Opinionated**                   | Provides pre-set defaults (but you can override) |
| **Convention over Configuration** | If you follow standard structure, it just works  |

Boot scans current package & subpackages for components.

Bean Creation & Dependency Injection
| Annotation                        | Purpose                                   |
| --------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------- |
| `@Component`                      | Generic bean managed by Spring IoC        |
| `@Service`                        | Business-logic bean                       |
| `@Repository`                     | DAO bean — also translates JPA exceptions |
| `@Controller` / `@RestController` | Handles web requests                      |
| `@Autowired`                      | Injects dependency automatically          |

@Component
| Concept             | Explanation                                                 |
| ------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------- |
| **Purpose**         | Marks a class as a Spring-managed bean                      |
| **Discovered by**   | `@ComponentScan`                                            |
| **Default scope**   | Singleton                                                   |
| **Bean name**       | Class name with first letter lowercase                      |
| **Common subtypes** | `@Service`, `@Repository`, `@Controller`, `@RestController` |
| **Benefit**         | No need to define beans manually in XML or Java config      |


@Value
| Concept           | Explanation                                                |
| ----------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------- |
| **Purpose**       | Inject values from properties/environment into Java fields |
| **Syntax**        | `@Value("${property.key}")`                                |
| **Default value** | `@Value("${property.key:defaultValue}")`                   |
| **Supports**      | Strings, numbers, booleans, lists, expressions             |
| **When to use**   | For small, specific configuration values                   |




@ComponentScan:
| Aspect          | Description                                                  |
| --------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------ |
| Purpose         | Scans for Spring-managed beans                               |
| Default in Boot | Automatically enabled via `@SpringBootApplication`           |
| Default scope   | Same package as main class + subpackages                     |
| Needed when     | Your beans are outside default package                       |
| Detects         | `@Component`, `@Service`, `@Repository`, `@Controller`, etc. |

Bean Naming

By default, Spring uses the class name (camelCase) as the bean name.
@Component
public class MessageService { ... }

You can override it:
@Component("customMessageService")
public class MessageService { ... }

Then you can autowire it using that name:
@Autowired
@Qualifier("customMessageService")
private MessageService messageService;

You can also control bean scope (singleton, prototype, etc.):
@Component
@Scope("prototype")
public class RandomNumberGenerator { ... }

Singleton vs Prototype

| Scope                   | Description                                                                                |
| ----------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |
| **Singleton** (default) | Only **one instance** of the bean is created and shared across the entire Spring container |
| **Prototype**           | A **new instance** is created **every time** the bean is requested from the container      |

| Feature                   | `singleton`                               | `prototype`                                                   |
| ------------------------- | ----------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------- |
| **Bean instances**        | One shared instance                       | New instance each time                                        |
| **Lifecycle managed by**  | Fully managed by Spring container         | Created by Spring, but destruction is caller’s responsibility |
| **Memory usage**          | Low (one instance)                        | Higher (many instances)                                       |
| **Thread-safety concern** | Not thread-safe by default (since shared) | Safer if each thread gets its own instance                    |
| **Default scope**         | ✅ Yes                                     | ❌ No                                                          |
| **Typical usage**         | Services, Repositories, Controllers       | Stateful beans, non-thread-safe helpers, temporary objects    |



@ResponseEntity is a wrapper for the entire HTTP response —
not just the body, but also the status code and headers.

So instead of returning just the data,
you can also control how the data is sent back to the client.

Why do we use ResponseEntity?
Customize HTTP status codes (e.g., 201 Created, 404 Not Found, 400 Bad Request)
Add custom headers (e.g., Location, Authorization, etc.)
Return empty responses (like 204 No Content)
Handle errors or exceptions cleanly
Improve clarity for REST APIs

ResponseEntity Methods

| Method                        | Description     | Example                                               |
| ----------------------------- | --------------- | ----------------------------------------------------- |
| `.ok(body)`                   | 200 OK          | `ResponseEntity.ok(employee)`                         |
| `.status(HttpStatus.CREATED)` | 201 Created     | `ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.CREATED).body(emp)` |
| `.notFound()`                 | 404 Not Found   | `ResponseEntity.notFound().build()`                   |
| `.noContent()`                | 204 No Content  | `ResponseEntity.noContent().build()`                  |
| `.badRequest()`               | 400 Bad Request | `ResponseEntity.badRequest().body("Invalid ID")`      |


| Good Practice              | Example                                  |
| -------------------------- | ---------------------------------------- |
| Use proper verbs           | `GET`, `POST`, `PUT`, `DELETE`           |
| Meaningful status codes    | `200 OK`, `201 Created`, `404 Not Found` |
| Consistent naming          | `/api/employees/{id}`                    |
| Return data + message      | Wrap response in DTO if needed           |
| Handle exceptions globally | Use your Phase 1 `@ControllerAdvice`     |
